config:
schema_version: 1
identity:
  model: gru
  version: v019
  parent: gru_v011
  status: running
description: 'The loss fix alone: globally-pooled weighted Pearson with a running
  statistic, everything else exactly as v011.'
hypothesis: 'The single most important fix in this repo''s history, and it was sitting
  in plain sight. This repo trained nineteen versions on a loss whose own docstring
  lied: chunk_loss() accepted a `state` argument and claimed it ''carries the moments
  across chunks so the statistic is cumulative rather than restarted''. The body never
  read `state`, and the call site never passed it. The weighted-Pearson statistic
  was therefore recomputed from each chunk''s own rows and restarted 20 times per
  sequence.


  That is exactly the failure dozken/wunder documents and calls out by name: a per-chunk
  Pearson subtracts a weighted mean estimated from that chunk alone, so the slow level
  component the metric actually scores is discarded and any per-chunk level offset
  is free. Their chunk4k runs ''win within-sequence correlation and lose on level
  drift'', and models trained on their chunk loss get WORSE with more training (0.575
  -> 0.553) instead of better. Their fix -- a running detached sufficient statistic
  pooled over the batch as well as over time -- is the 2026-09-15 metric, which pools
  every scored row of every sequence into ONE correlation per target.


  Expected: a large gain, larger than anything else we have tried, because every prior
  run was optimising a surrogate that structurally cannot see the quantity being scored.
  Our +0.058 over the organiser baseline came from the soft mask and the state reset
  on top of a mis-specified loss; with the loss correct those two should compound
  rather than merely survive.'
change_vs_parent: 'ONE change off gru_v011: the loss. chunk_loss now keeps a running
  detached sufficient statistic [sum w, sum wy, sum wp, sum wy^2, sum wp^2, sum wyp]
  pooled over BOTH the batch and the time axis into one value per target, reset every
  stats_window steps (default one sequence''s worth of chunks). The previous implementation
  recomputed the statistic from each chunk''s own rows and discarded it, despite accepting
  a `state` argument it never read.

  Unchanged: the soft mask, state_reset 4000, chunk_len 2000, aux weighted-MSE 0.05,
  EMA 0.999, architecture 2x128 proj0 (192,258 params), seed 1337, 3-epoch cosine
  schedule, data (train shard only).'
